    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d387">

                <pb facs="0495" n="481" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg481"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/7–2144: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-07-21T14:00:00+07:00">July 21, 1944—2
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received July 21—1:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1257. Wang Shih-chieh told the Counselor last evening that no progress is being
                    made in negotiations with the Communists. (Paragraph 3 our 1158, July 4<anchor
                        n="8" xml:id="d387-f1"/>.) Latter’s representative here stating that
                    anticipated instructions from Yenan have not been received. Wang expressed
                    opinion that Communist leaders were waiting outcome of Hunan fighting before
                    making reply to Generalissimo’s “counter proposals”.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
